<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The July 13 gain of 9.14% followed the breakdown of the June transit memorandum; the August 3 and August 4 declines followed Washington&#8217;s decision to delay planned strikes and Omani mediation of a transit arrangement. Source: Investing.com daily settlements.</p>
<p>For a Canadian portfolio the exposure is not confined to the energy sleeve. Statistics Canada reported on Tuesday that total exports rose 13.1% in the second quarter, the strongest quarterly gain in percentage terms since the third quarter of 2020, with almost half of that increase coming from energy products on higher prices. The same oil price that sits inside the equity allocation also sits inside the national accounts and, through the terms of trade, inside the currency.</p>
<p>The Canadian dollar traded at 71.08 cents US on Tuesday, down from 71.28 cents on Friday. A Canadian holding a domestic balanced portfolio is therefore exposed to the Hormuz headline three times over: in the equity sleeve, in the currency, and in the macro data that will shape the Bank of Canada&#8217;s September 2 decision.</p>
<p>That concentration is what converts a well-documented judgment error into a portfolio outcome. In a market where a single headline can move the relevant price by 6% before lunch, the cost of substituting the vivid case for the historical frequency is not a rounding error. It is the difference between holding a position through a fourth round trip and selling into the bottom of one.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They&#8217;re Feeling</div>
<p>Relief with an undertow of suspicion. Clients who have watched energy holdings swing for five months are reading Tuesday&#8217;s headlines as the end of something, and they want that to be true. Clients with concentrated Canadian energy exposure are quietly calculating what a genuine reopening does to their position and are reluctant to say so out loud. Clients who sold energy in late June, when crude touched pre-war levels, and watched it rally 27% in July are carrying regret they have not mentioned. That unspoken regret is the most likely driver of a poor decision this week.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Crude is down 11% over two sessions on progress toward a Hormuz transit arrangement. I want to give you the number that matters more than that one. Over the last twenty-two trading sessions, oil moved more than 2% on twelve of them. That is not a supply market. That is a market repricing the odds of a diplomatic outcome every time an official speaks. We have seen this exact sequence before. In late June, crude fell back to where it sat before the war started, on a transit memorandum that looked solid. Two weeks later it was back up and by July 23 it was at $87. I am not telling you this deal will fail. I have no idea. I am telling you that the market has now made this trade four times this year and unwound it four times, and I do not want us making a permanent decision on a position we hold for years based on where we are inside one of those swings. If your energy weighting was right for you in June, it is still right for you today. If it was too high in June, then it was too high, and today&#8217;s price is a better one to fix it at than the one we had two weeks ago. Which of those two is it? Let us decide that on the weighting, not on the headline.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who&#8217;s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated Canadian energy positions, clients holding energy-weighted sector funds, and retirees drawing income from Canadian energy dividends who have watched distributions ride an oil price that has round-tripped four times.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Holders of broad Canadian balanced portfolios, where energy exposure is diluted but currency exposure and Bank of Canada rate sensitivity move in the same direction as the oil price.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with meaningful US-dollar or international allocations, clients holding gold and precious metals producers, and clients on the buy side of the Canadian consumer sector, for whom lower crude is a cost input rather than a revenue line.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull the energy sector weighting for every discretionary account and compare it to target, before any client calls you.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients who reduced energy exposure in the last week of June and check whether they re-entered. These are the accounts most exposed to a regret-driven decision this week.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document any client-directed trade made on the Hormuz headline this week, including the client&#8217;s stated reasoning, in the file notes.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review Canadian dollar exposure in accounts with US-dollar liabilities or planned US spending, given the loonie at 71.08 cents.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare the Friday labour force survey and September 2 Bank of Canada dates for clients who ask what comes next.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Oil down 11% in two days: the number behind the headline<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
You have likely seen that crude fell sharply Monday and Tuesday on reports of progress toward a Strait of Hormuz transit arrangement. West Texas Intermediate settled at $75.23, down 11.2% from Friday.<br><br>
One piece of context before anyone draws a conclusion. Over the past twenty-two trading sessions, oil has moved 2% or more on twelve of them. That is a market revising its estimate of a diplomatic outcome, not one absorbing news about physical supply. A very similar sequence played out in late June, when crude fell back to pre-war levels on a transit memorandum. By July 23 it had recovered to $87.<br><br>
This may be the round that holds. I have no way to know, and neither does anyone quoting a price target this morning. What I can tell you is that your allocation was built for a range of outcomes rather than one of them, and nothing in the last forty-eight hours changes the reasoning behind it.<br><br>
If you would like to review your energy weighting on its own merits, rather than in reaction to this week, I am glad to do that. Reply here or call and we will book time.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors with concentrated Canadian energy exposure.</strong> The single most exposed group this week. They built the position on a thesis about the Hormuz closure, they have watched it round-trip four times since February, and they have no framework for distinguishing a genuine regime change from the fourth false start. They also have nobody to call at 6 a.m. when the front month is down 6%.</p>
<p><strong>Recently retired professionals drawing from a Canadian dividend portfolio.</strong> Canadian dividend income is heavily concentrated in banks and energy. This group feels every oil headline in their income line and generally has no formal drawdown policy that survives contact with a volatile quarter.</p>
<p><strong>Incorporated business owners with corporate investment accounts.</strong> Often heavily home-biased, often energy-heavy, and rarely reviewed against a stated risk target because nobody is formally responsible for doing it.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because oil is down 11% in two sessions and I suspect you are looking at your energy position this morning wondering whether this is the real reopening or the fourth false one. I do not have the answer to that. What I do have is the count of how many times this exact trade has been made and unwound since February, and I have found that number changes how people think about the decision. Would that be useful to you?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The asymmetry in this moment is unusually clean. A self-directed investor holding Canadian energy has spent five months making a series of binary calls on a geopolitical negotiation they have no special insight into, using the only information available to them, which is the same headline everyone else read. Each of those calls felt like analysis. Collectively they are a sequence of coin flips executed at the worst possible moments, because the moment a decision feels most urgent is the moment the price has already moved.</p>
<p>An advisor does not add value here by predicting the Hormuz outcome. The advisor adds value by holding the base rate in view when the client cannot, by having the position sizing conversation before the headline rather than during it, and by converting a five-month sequence of reactive trades into one deliberate decision about how much of this risk the portfolio should carry at all. That is a service a screen cannot render, and this week is when its absence is most obvious.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How closely have you been following the Hormuz negotiations over the past few weeks?</p>
<p>When crude fell back to pre-war levels in late June, did you make any changes to your holdings?</p>
<p>What proportion of your portfolio sits in Canadian energy today, and how does that compare with where you intended it to be?</p>
<p>If a transit agreement is signed this week and crude settles in the sixties, what would you do on Thursday morning?</p>
<p>Who do you talk to before you make a decision like that?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Twelve of the last twenty-two oil sessions<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
Crude fell 11.2% across Monday and Tuesday on reports of progress toward a Strait of Hormuz transit arrangement. If you hold Canadian energy, you have probably already looked.<br><br>
Here is the statistic I have been sharing with clients this week. Over the last twenty-two trading sessions, oil moved 2% or more on twelve of them, and 4% or more on eight. That is not a market responding to supply. It is a market re-estimating the odds of a diplomatic outcome every time an official speaks, and it has now made and unwound this same trade four times since February.<br><br>
That does not tell you what to do. It does tell you that decisions made inside one of these swings tend to look different in hindsight than they did at the time.<br><br>
If you would find it useful to look at your energy exposure as a sizing question rather than a timing one, I am happy to spend twenty minutes on it with no obligation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
