</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Bank of Canada, monthly average foreign exchange rates, August 2025 to July 2026. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Figures are US cents per Canadian dollar, calculated from the Bank of Canada&#8217;s published monthly average CAD/USD rate. Statistics Canada separately reported the May-to-June move as the largest monthly decline in the average value of the dollar since October 2022. Source: Bank of Canada.</p>
<p>A client who has been converting CAD to USD steadily through 2026, whether to fund a US brokerage account, cover cross-border spending, or build a corporate USD operating balance, has been doing so at a meaningfully less favourable rate in June and July than in February through May. That has two direct tax consequences, and they are not the same consequence.</p>
<h2>Two Different CRA Rules, Not One</h2>
<p>Subsection 39(2) of the Income Tax Act governs foreign exchange gains and losses realised on capital account. For individuals, the first $200 CAD of a net annual foreign exchange gain or loss is exempt. This exemption applies specifically to gains and losses arising from currency conversion itself: converting CAD to USD and back, or using USD cash to make a purchase or payment.</p>
<p>It does not apply to foreign exchange embedded in the sale of a foreign-denominated security. When a client sells a US-listed stock held in a non-registered account, the capital gain or loss is calculated by converting both the proceeds and the adjusted cost base to CAD at their respective transaction dates, and the resulting figure, currency movement included, is a single capital gain or loss with no $200 threshold attached. Advisors who apply the cash-conversion exemption to a securities disposition are applying a rule that does not reach that transaction.</p>
<h2>What Non-Registered and Corporate Accounts Owe That Registered Accounts Do Not</h2>
<p>Account type determines whether any of this matters at all. Inside an RRSP, RRIF, TFSA, or FHSA, currency movement has no personal tax consequence, since income and gains inside these accounts are sheltered or tax-deferred regardless of what the loonie does. The exposure sits entirely in non-registered personal accounts and in corporate investment or operating accounts.</p>
<p>For a CCPC holding US-dollar operating cash or a US-denominated investment account, the $200 exemption does not apply at all. Subsection 39(2)&#8217;s de minimis threshold is written for individuals. A corporation realises a foreign exchange gain or loss on every dollar of movement between acquisition and disposition, with no floor, taxed at the standard capital gains inclusion rate of 50 percent, unchanged after the 2024 proposal to raise it was cancelled in March 2025.</p>
<h2>The T1135 Threshold Moves With the Rate, Not Against It</h2>
<p>Form T1135 requires Canadian resident individuals, corporations, and certain trusts to report specified foreign property once its total cost amount exceeds $100,000 CAD at any point in the year. For property acquired in US dollars, that cost amount is the CAD value at the exchange rate on the date of acquisition, translated once and left alone. A weaker loonie today does not retroactively raise the CAD cost amount of a US holding bought in February at 73.25 cents.</p>
<p>What it does change is the arithmetic on every new purchase made from June onward. A client contributing the same number of US dollars to a US brokerage account in July converted at 70.89 cents rather than February&#8217;s 73.25, meaning the same USD contribution now books a larger CAD cost amount than it would have five months earlier. For a client dollar-cost-averaging into US equities through the back half of 2026, the cumulative T1135 threshold arrives sooner in USD terms than it would have in the spring, purely on the strength of the rate at which each contribution was converted.</p>
<h2>The Planning Bridge Is Timing, Not Waiting</h2>
<p>None of this is urgent in the sense of a filing deadline. It is urgent in the sense that adjusted cost base is a running total, tracked transaction by transaction at the settlement date, and a five-month gap in a client&#8217;s USD account activity is a five-month gap in the ACB record that has to be reconstructed later, usually during tax season, usually under time pressure, and usually from incomplete brokerage statements.</p>
<p>The advisors best positioned this fall are the ones who pull the USD account activity for clients likely to be affected now, while June and July&#8217;s conversions are still fresh in the client&#8217;s memory and the brokerage records are current, rather than waiting for a T3 or T5 slip to surface the question in March.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They&#8217;re Feeling</div>
<p>Mild confusion more than anything. Most clients with USD accounts have noticed the loonie is weaker without connecting it to a tax consequence. Clients who run a small business through a CCPC and hold USD operating cash tend to be the most anxious once the exemption gap is explained, because they assumed the same $200 rule applied to their corporation as to their personal account.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Statistics Canada flagged this week that the loonie had its largest monthly drop since October 2022, and the Bank of Canada&#8217;s own numbers show it has kept softening into July. If you hold a US-dollar account or US securities outside a registered plan, that move has a tax dimension worth fifteen minutes of our time. There are two separate rules here, not one. If you have been converting cash between Canadian and US dollars, the first $200 of gain or loss in a year is exempt. If you have been selling US stocks in a non-registered account, that exemption does not apply, the currency movement is baked into the capital gain calculation with no floor. And if this is running through your corporation rather than personally, there is no $200 exemption at all, every dollar of movement counts. None of this needs a decision today. What it needs is for us to pull your account activity from the last few months while it is still easy to reconstruct, so nothing gets missed when your return is prepared.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who&#8217;s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Incorporated business owners with CCPC operating or investment accounts holding US-dollar cash, where no $200 exemption applies to any foreign exchange movement.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Individuals with non-registered US-dollar accounts or US-listed securities who have been actively converting currency or trading through the summer.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients whose USD holdings sit entirely inside RRSP, RRIF, TFSA, or FHSA accounts, where currency movement carries no personal tax consequence regardless of the rate.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify every client with a non-registered USD account or a CCPC holding USD cash, before reaching for the phone.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull June and July account statements for those clients while the transaction detail is still current in the brokerage record.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm which clients are making regular USD contributions and flag any approaching the $100,000 CAD cost amount for T1135 purposes.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Separate, in every client file, cash-conversion transactions (subject to the $200 exemption) from securities dispositions (not subject to it).</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note corporate accounts explicitly: no $200 exemption applies, full FX gain or loss is included at the standard rate.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your USD account and this week&#8217;s currency data<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Statistics Canada reported this week that the Canadian dollar posted its largest monthly decline since October 2022 in June, and Bank of Canada data shows it has softened further into July, averaging 70.89 cents US against 73.25 cents in February.<br><br>
If you hold a non-registered US-dollar account, US securities, or run USD activity through your corporation, this move has a tax dimension. The rules differ depending on the account: personal cash conversions carry a $200 exemption on the first bit of gain or loss each year, securities dispositions do not carry that exemption at all, and corporate accounts have no exemption whatsoever.<br><br>
I would like to review your account activity from the past few months while the records are current, rather than reconstructing it later. This is a five to ten minute conversation, not a change to anything you are currently doing. Let me know a good time to connect.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Incorporated business owners with US-dollar operating accounts.</strong> This group is the most exposed and least aware. Business owners moving USD through a CCPC for supplier payments or US revenue rarely realise the $200 personal exemption has no corporate equivalent, and rarely have anyone tracking the ACB on that cash pool at all.</p>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors with non-registered US brokerage accounts.</strong> DIY investors who have been trading US equities through 2026 are sitting on a currency-embedded capital gain or loss calculation they likely have not separated from their security-level gains, and have no advisor checking the arithmetic.</p>
<p><strong>Clients dollar-cost-averaging into US holdings from a non-registered account.</strong> A systematic USD contribution plan is exactly the kind of activity that quietly crosses the T1135 threshold sooner in a weak-CAD stretch, without the client noticing until a slip arrives.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because Statistics Canada flagged this week that the Canadian dollar just had its largest monthly drop since October 2022, and if you are holding US dollars or US securities outside a registered account, that has a tax tracking dimension most people do not have anyone watching for them. Do you know offhand whether your US-dollar activity this year has been tracked at the transaction level, or is it something you would be reconstructing at tax time?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed investor or business owner managing their own US-dollar exposure is, in effect, running a parallel bookkeeping obligation they did not sign up for. Every currency conversion, every US securities trade, and every corporate USD payment carries an adjusted cost base calculation under subsection 39(2), and the rules differ meaningfully by account type and transaction type in ways that are easy to get backwards without noticing.</p>
<p>The advisor&#8217;s value here is not predicting where the loonie goes next. It is ensuring the record exists before it needs to, so that a T1135 filing obligation or a foreign exchange gain calculation is a known quantity rather than a scramble discovered from a brokerage statement in March. That is a service a self-directed platform does not provide and a DIY investor rarely replicates on their own.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you currently hold US-dollar cash or US securities outside a registered account?</p>
<p>If you run a business, does it hold or transact in US dollars through a corporate account?</p>
<p>Has anyone been tracking the adjusted cost base on your US-dollar holdings as the rate has moved this year?</p>
<p>Do you know roughly what the total cost amount of your foreign property is, relative to the $100,000 T1135 reporting threshold?</p>
<p>Who currently prepares your return, and do they have visibility into your account-level currency activity or only the year-end summary?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The loonie&#8217;s worst month since 2022, and your USD account<br><br>
