<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Goods-producing industries rose 0.6% overall and services 0.2%; the seven sectors shown accounted for most of the dispersion around that average. Source: Statistics Canada.</p>
<p>Mining, oil and gas extraction led with a 1.0% monthly gain, the strongest of any sector Statistics Canada tracked. Construction added 0.8% and utilities 0.7%. On the softer side, agriculture contracted 0.9%, the one clear drag among goods-producing industries. Services grew more modestly overall at 0.2%, with real estate at 0.4% and transportation and warehousing at 0.3% doing most of the lifting.</p>
<h2>The Energy Channel Is the Same One Running Through Everything Else This Week</h2>
<p>The 1.0% gain in mining, oil and gas is not an isolated data point. Statistics Canada&#8217;s June trade release, published Tuesday, showed total exports rose 13.1% in the second quarter, the strongest quarterly increase since the third quarter of 2020, and attributed almost half of that gain to higher exports of energy products, driven in large part by elevated prices tied to the conflict in the Middle East.</p>
<p>That is the same channel putting upward pressure on the loonie&#8217;s trade math this week and the same channel behind Tuesday&#8217;s 6% single-session decline in WTI crude on Hormuz transit optimism. A GDP print that looks purely domestic is, underneath, running through the same oil price that is moving three other desks&#8217; stories today.</p>
<h2>Core Inflation Is Not Confirming the Growth Story, and That Is the Point</h2>
<p>Strong, broad-based growth is usually the kind of print that makes a central bank nervous about inflation. This one arrived alongside the opposite signal. The Bank of Canada&#8217;s preferred core inflation measures, trim and median, fell to 1.8% and 1.9% respectively in June, each down two-tenths from May&#8217;s 2.0% and 2.1%, and each at its lowest level in over five years.</p>
<p>Headline CPI told a noisier version of the same story, falling to 2.8% in June from May&#8217;s 3.2%, itself the fastest headline print since December 2023. The swing in both directions traced almost entirely to gasoline, which decelerated sharply as an interim Middle East easing brought wholesale fuel prices down. Strip out energy&#8217;s volatility and the underlying trend the Bank actually targets is cooling, not accelerating, even as the real economy outruns the Bank&#8217;s own forecast.</p>
<h2>What This Combination Means for September 2</h2>
<p>The Bank of Canada has held its overnight rate at 2.25% for six consecutive decisions, most recently on July 15. The next announcement is September 2, and the data assembled this week argues for continuity rather than a change in either direction.</p>
<p>A growth beat of this size would ordinarily build a case for the Bank to stay firmly on hold, or even lean hawkish, guarding against an overheating economy. But core inflation easing to a five-year low removes the inflationary urgency that beat would otherwise create, giving the Bank room to keep holding without needing to defend the decision against a rising-price backdrop. The bond market&#8217;s own read leans the same way: the Government of Canada 10-year yield eased to 3.57% on Tuesday, down nine basis points on the session, consistent with a market pricing continuity rather than a hawkish surprise.</p>
<p>The next data point arrives before the Bank does. Statistics Canada releases the July Labour Force Survey on Friday, August 7, with RBC Economics forecasting a modest gain of 5,000 jobs and the unemployment rate holding at 6.5%. A labour market that holds its current shape, alongside this week&#8217;s growth and inflation data, would leave the Bank with little reason to move on September 2 in either direction, which is itself the more useful piece of planning information than a directional rate call would be.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They&#8217;re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients renewing a mortgage in the next six to twelve months are the most attentive to this data, and what they are feeling is impatience: hoping strong growth data means nothing changes so their current rate expectations hold, while quietly worried that any sign of an overheating economy could push the Bank toward a less accommodative stance than they were counting on.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Two pieces of data came out this week that usually pull the Bank of Canada in opposite directions, and this time they are not fighting each other. The economy grew faster in the second quarter than the Bank itself forecast, which on its own would normally raise concerns about inflation picking back up. At the same time, the Bank''s own preferred measures of core inflation fell to their lowest level in over five years in June. Strong growth without inflation pressure is close to the best combination a central bank can see, and it points toward the Bank staying exactly where it is at 2.25% on September 2 rather than moving in either direction. That is not a guarantee, and Friday''s jobs report will matter too, but nothing in this week''s data gives the Bank a reason to change course. For your renewal specifically, that argues for treating current rate levels as the base case rather than waiting for a cut that this data does not currently support.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who&#8217;s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with mortgages renewing in the next two quarters who have been delaying a rate decision in hopes of a Bank of Canada cut.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Fixed-income holders and GIC ladder clients, for whom continuity at 2.25% supports current yields without the upside of a hike or the reinvestment risk of a cut.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Equity holders in growth-sensitive Canadian sectors, particularly energy and materials, where the same trade and GDP data reinforcing the September 2 policy read also reflects real revenue strength.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients with mortgage renewals inside the next two quarters for a rate-expectations conversation before September 2.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review fixed-income and GIC ladder positioning against a continued-hold scenario rather than a cut scenario.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note Friday, August 7''s Labour Force Survey as the next data point clients will ask about; have RBC''s 5,000-job, 6.5%-unemployment forecast ready for context.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Cross-reference energy-sector holdings against the GDP sector data, since the same oil price move is showing up in growth, trade, and currency data this week.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What this week''s growth and inflation data means for September 2<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Two data points from Statistics Canada this week are worth flagging together. Second-quarter economic growth is tracking meaningfully above the Bank of Canada''s own forecast, while the Bank''s preferred measures of core inflation fell to a five-year low in June.<br><br>
Strong growth without inflation pressure is an unusually clean combination, and it points toward the Bank of Canada holding its overnight rate at 2.25% on September 2 rather than moving in either direction. Friday''s July jobs report will add one more piece to that picture.<br><br>
If your planning has been built around the possibility of a rate cut this fall, this week''s data argues for treating a continued hold as the more likely base case. I am happy to walk through what that means for your specific situation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Homeowners with a mortgage renewing in the next six months.</strong> This group is actively searching for rate guidance right now, and most are getting it from generic bank rate pages rather than anyone connecting the underlying growth and inflation data to what the Bank of Canada is likely to do next.</p>
<p><strong>DIY fixed-income investors managing their own GIC or bond ladder.</strong> A hold-not-cut environment changes reinvestment timing decisions, and self-directed investors typically have no framework for reading a growth-versus-inflation data week like this one.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with variable-rate financing.</strong> Owners with floating-rate credit lines or commercial mortgages are directly exposed to the September 2 decision and often have no one interpreting the incoming data for them ahead of it.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because this week''s Canadian growth and inflation data gave a fairly clear read on what the Bank of Canada is likely to do on September 2, and I suspect that is relevant to a decision you may be sitting on, whether that is a mortgage renewal or how you are positioning fixed income. Do you have a few minutes to talk through what the data is actually saying?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed client watching for the next Bank of Canada move typically reads headlines in isolation: a strong GDP print in one story, a soft inflation print in another, with no framework connecting them. This week the two point the same direction, toward continuity, and that is a genuinely useful, actionable read that most people managing their own finances will not construct on their own from the raw data releases.</p>
<p>The advisor''s value is not predicting the Bank''s decision with certainty. It is synthesizing what the incoming data actually implies, in time for it to inform a renewal, a reinvestment, or a financing decision before the decision date arrives, rather than reacting to the announcement after the fact.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you have a mortgage or line of credit renewing in the next six to twelve months?</p>
<p>Have you been waiting on a Bank of Canada rate cut before making a financing or investment decision?</p>
<p>How is your fixed-income exposure currently positioned, and against what rate scenario?</p>
<p>Who is currently helping you interpret Bank of Canada data as it comes out?</p>
<p>What would change about your plan if the Bank held rates through the rest of 2026?</p>
</div>
