VALUES (
  '2026/08/05/hormuz-temporary-corridor-deal-not-reopening',
  'geo',
  'article',
  'The Market Rallied on a Strait of Hormuz Deal. The Deal Itself Is a 60-Day Negotiation Over How Much of the Strait Iran Keeps.',
  'Tuesday''s TSX rally and 6.36% WTI decline priced Hormuz optimism as though the strait were reopening. What Iranian, American, and Omani officials are actually describing is a temporary, Iran-administered corridor with a stated one-to-three-month shelf life, and the two sides do not agree on its terms.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The TSX rallied 1.63% Tuesday as WTI fell 6.36%,</strong><span> now down 13.6% from its July 23 peak of $87.01, on Strait of Hormuz optimism.</span></li>
<li><strong>What is actually on the table is a 60-day negotiating window,</strong><span> not a reopening, per Iranian, American, and Omani officials cited by the New York Times and Oman Observer.</span></li>
<li><strong>Iran''s own negotiators describe a one to three month arrangement,</strong><span> in which Iran controls security, demining, and maritime services in the strait.</span></li>
<li><strong>A US official disputed Iran''s characterization of the talks Monday,</strong><span> saying it was not accurate, a direct contradiction between the two sides.</span></li>
<li><strong>The June 17 memorandum collapsed within three weeks,</strong><span> the closest precedent for how this year''s Hormuz de-escalations have resolved.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The S&amp;P/TSX composite closed Tuesday up 575.45 points, 1.63%, its strongest session in weeks, on what wire reports described as optimism the Strait of Hormuz could soon reopen. WTI crude fell 6.36% the same session, down 13.6% from its July 23 peak of $87.01.</p>
<p>The trade connecting those two moves is straightforward: a reopened strait means restored supply, restored supply means lower oil, and Canadian equities not tied to energy rallied on the relief. What is less straightforward, and worth tracing carefully before extending that trade, is what is actually being negotiated in Muscat this week.</p>
<h2>What the Market Priced Versus What Is on the Table</h2>
<p>According to Iranian, American, and Omani officials cited by the New York Times and the Oman Observer on Tuesday, the emerging arrangement gives Iran and Oman 60 days to negotiate the strait''s opening. During that window, ships heading into the Gulf would transit a channel controlled by Iran and close to its coast; ships leaving would use a channel near Oman''s. Iran would charge no formal toll but a "service fee" covering security, environmental impact, and staffing.</p>
<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
