<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The red band marks Washington''s July 8 declaration that the June 17 memorandum of understanding was over; the green band marks the current Oman-Iran corridor talks that began Monday. Source: Investing.com.</p>
<p>Separately, a member of Iran''s negotiating committee told state broadcaster IRIB on Tuesday that the goal is an arrangement lasting one to three months in which Iran is, in his words, dominant: security, demining, and maritime services all handled by Iran. Reuters reported Tuesday that Iran wants control over inbound shipping and visibility over outbound traffic, with the ability to intervene if it judges necessary.</p>
<h2>Washington and Tehran Are Not Describing the Same Deal</h2>
<p>A US official told reporters Monday that the Iranian characterization of the talks was, in that official''s words, not accurate, and said any temporary route would not involve Iranian approvals or permissions, and would carry no tolls. That is a direct contradiction of what Iran''s own negotiators were telling their own state media the next day.</p>
<p>Inside the Pentagon, officials briefed on the talks have reportedly voiced concern the arrangement amounts to a concession that ratifies Iranian control over what was, before February, an open international waterway. Extensive mines reportedly remain in the strait regardless of which flag is on the negotiating table, meaning vessels will need to coordinate with Iran to transit safely even on the route the United States has been protecting.</p>
<h2>The Base Rate This Market Has Not Yet Priced</h2>
<p>This is not the first time in 2026 that a Hormuz de-escalation has been priced as durable. The June 17 memorandum of understanding ending the initial war produced a similar relief rally before Washington declared it over on July 8 and resumed strikes, sending WTI up 4.4% in a single session. President Trump has predicted the strait could reopen "literally by tomorrow" on multiple occasions since the war began in February, a claim that has not yet been correct.</p>
<p>None of that means the current talks will fail. It means the base rate for an announced Hormuz de-escalation converting into a durable resolution, rather than a time-limited arrangement that reopens the same question in one to three months, has not been strong this year. A structure both sides are describing as temporary, with a negotiated shelf life stated by Iran''s own side, is not the same analytical category as a settled reopening, whatever the market did with it Tuesday.</p>
<h2>The Canadian Chain: From Corridor Control to Q3 Export Revenue</h2>
<p>Statistics Canada''s June trade release, published Tuesday, showed second-quarter exports rising 13.1%, the strongest quarterly gain since the third quarter of 2020, with almost half of that increase tied to elevated energy prices flowing directly from this conflict. That tailwind, and the trade surplus and GDP contribution built on it, is a function of exactly the risk premium this week''s talks are trying to remove.</p>
<p>If the arrangement now under negotiation is the narrow, Iran-administered, one-to-three-month structure both Iranian officials and Pentagon sources describe, rather than the clean reopening equity markets priced Tuesday, the risk premium supporting Canadian energy revenue is more likely to prove conditional than resolved. A Canadian portfolio with meaningful energy-sector weighting is exposed to that gap between what was priced and what was actually agreed to, and the exposure runs in both directions: a genuine reopening compresses energy-sector revenue from here, while a fifth round-trip on the same narrow arrangement leaves the current price support, and the export and GDP data built on it, more fragile than this week''s headlines suggest.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They&#8217;re Feeling</div>
<p>Relief, largely undifferentiated. Clients saw the TSX rally and oil fall and are reading it as the conflict ending, without distinguishing between a full reopening and the narrower, time-limited arrangement actually being negotiated. Clients with concentrated energy holdings are quietly wondering whether Tuesday''s move means they should trim, without a framework for judging how durable it is.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Tuesday''s rally was real, and it was driven by genuine progress in the Hormuz talks. But I want to be precise about what is actually being negotiated, because it matters for how much weight to put on the move. What is on the table right now is not a reopening in the pre-war sense. It is a 60-day negotiating window for a corridor that Iran would control on the inbound side, described by Iran''s own negotiators as an arrangement where Iran is, in their words, dominant, for one to three months. The US side disputes parts of how Iran is characterizing it. That is not a settled resolution, it is a temporary structure with a stated shelf life, and this year has already seen one very similar arrangement, the June memorandum, collapse within three weeks of being signed. I am not telling you to ignore Tuesday''s move. I am telling you it is one data point in a pattern that has reversed several times, and I do not want us treating a single strong session as confirmation that the underlying risk is gone.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who&#8217;s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated Canadian energy holdings whose recent returns have been supported by the same price premium this week''s talks are trying to remove.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Broad Canadian balanced portfolios, where energy weighting is diluted but the trade and GDP data behind this quarter''s strength runs through the same channel.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients positioned in Canadian consumer or transportation names sensitive to input costs, where a genuine, durable reopening would be a net tailwind regardless of how the current talks resolve.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review energy-sector weighting against target before treating Tuesday''s move as a signal to act.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients who reduced energy exposure after the June MOU and re-entered after its July 8 collapse, since they are the most likely to react again on this news.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document the distinction between a full reopening and the temporary corridor structure in any client-facing notes this week.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Monitor for confirmation or denial from Tehran and Washington over the next several sessions rather than treating Tuesday''s price action as final.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What Tuesday''s Hormuz rally actually priced<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Tuesday''s market move, the TSX up 1.63% and oil down over 6%, was driven by optimism around Strait of Hormuz talks. Before drawing conclusions from it, I want to flag what is actually being negotiated.<br><br>
Reports from multiple outlets describe a 60-day negotiating window for a corridor that Iran would control on the inbound side, with Iran''s own negotiators describing a one-to-three-month arrangement in which Iran is dominant. US officials dispute parts of that characterization. This is a temporary structure, not a settled reopening, and a very similar arrangement in June collapsed within three weeks.<br><br>
I am not suggesting you ignore the move. I am suggesting we treat it as one data point in a pattern this year has not yet resolved cleanly, and avoid making a permanent portfolio decision based on a single session.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors reacting to Tuesday''s headline without the underlying detail.</strong> Most DIY investors saw the TSX rally and oil drop and drew a simple conclusion. Few have read past the headline to see that what is being negotiated is a temporary, Iran-administered corridor rather than a reopening.</p>
<p><strong>Concentrated energy holders who have not revisited their weighting since February.</strong> Clients who built an energy position early in the conflict and have not systematically reviewed it against a defined target are carrying accumulated risk they may not have re-underwritten since the original thesis.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because markets rallied hard Tuesday on Hormuz optimism, and I want to make sure you have seen the detail behind the headline before deciding whether it changes anything for your energy exposure. Have you seen the specifics of what is actually being negotiated, or just the market reaction to it?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed investor reading Tuesday''s headlines has access to the same TSX close and oil price everyone else does. What they typically do not have is the discipline, or the sourcing, to check whether the underlying event matches the market''s reaction to it, and this is a week where the gap between the two is unusually wide: a temporary, disputed, 60-day arrangement being priced as though it were a resolution.</p>
<p>The advisor''s value is not calling the outcome of the Hormuz talks. It is ensuring the client''s position sizing reflects what has actually been agreed to, not what the headline implied, and revisiting that sizing at the moments, like this one, when the gap between the two is largest.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Did Tuesday''s market move affect how you are thinking about your energy holdings?</p>
<p>Have you reviewed your energy sector weighting since the conflict began in February?</p>
<p>What would you do if this week''s talks collapsed the way the June agreement did?</p>
<p>Who is helping you separate the headline from the underlying negotiating detail on stories like this one?</p>
</div>
